window.docContents[245].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Pull":{"contents":"The wobble throws body A's centre of mass off the line, and a second observer on a moving cart sees the pair differently. What must the model now say about where things are, and for whom? L4 moves the two centres of mass into Space 3, distinguishes points from vectors and distances, and states the observer whose frame supplies a vector's context. One name is one system: records under it are states at different times, configurations, or rival estimates; comparing them is the point. Distinct names are distinct systems. Pair P's separation has one bearer across its states, while pair Q has a different bearer. The lab frame F and cart frame G are explicit observers.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L4 — Out of the line","header":"5.5.3. Pull","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Pull"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-multi-threaded-___-coarse-grained-task-parallelism":{"contents":"Keep the scalar carrier and split the row-independent (pixel) axis into contiguous slices, running\nthe whole kernel on each slice on its own dedicated Lean Task. The parallelism is Lean's own task\nconstruct — no external threading library, no OpenMP shim — and it is coarse-grained: one task per\nslice runs the entire op-chain, so the compute-per-fork is maximal and per-operation fork/join\noverhead is nil. This is exact whenever the work-items are independent (which the per-pixel retrieval\nis), and it scales toward the core count when the model has good arithmetic intensity — enough\ncompute per work-item that the cores are kept busy rather than starved on memory bandwidth or throttled\nby task overhead. A model with poor intensity, or one whose items are not independent, sees little\nbenefit here and is better served by the single-threaded baseline. This strategy is the deployment\nlayer's runSharded.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009Go fast, automatically — the deployment spectrum","header":"18.3.2. CPU, multi-threaded — coarse-grained task parallelism","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-multi-threaded-___-coarse-grained-task-parallelism"}});